 { In this early endgame the initiative is definitely with White, whose immediate threat of Nxe4 can't be parried by simple means. Morozevich found a practical solution. } 1... { -KEY- } Qf7+ $1 ( { After } 1... e3 $6 2. Nxb7 $1 { both } Nxb7 3. Qc6 ( { and } 2... Qxb7 3. Rxe3 { lead to increased difficulties for Black. } ) 
 ) 
2. Qxf7+ Kxf7 3. Nxe4 Nxe4 4. Rxe4 { The picture has changed completely - White is no longer attacking but instead enjoys a material advantage. The reason why Morozevich wanted to reach this ending is simple: if we remove the queenside pawns from the board, then the ensuing position with rook and two pawns against bishop and two pawns is inside the boundaries of a theoretical draw. } b5 $1 { Forcing the exchange of the dangerous a4-pawn. } 5. Ke2 bxa4 6. Rxa4 a5 7. Rc4 Bg7 { The reason for this particular retreat will become evident two moves later. } 8. Rc7+ Kg8 9. Ra7 { Timman now plans Kf3-g4, followed by h3-h4-h5 with an unpleasant situation for Black: if he permits h5-h6, then the h7-pawn is fixed as a weakness; besides, a back-rank mate will be in the air once the white king arrives at e6. On the other hand, if he replies ...g6xh5, then after Kxh5 and g5-g6 at the right moment, White would have winning chances in the ensuing rook vs. bishop ending, considering that Black's king is located in one of the 'wrong' corners. } h6 $1 { A key decision. By giving up the a-pawn, which has no relevance anyway for the theoretical draw involved, Morozevich finishes off the enemy plan in advance. } 10. gxh6 ( { After } 10. h4 hxg5 11. hxg5 { the deployment of the bishop on the long diagonal will prevent the white king from reaching the crucial f6-square; e.g. } Bc3 12. Kd3 Bg7 13. Rxa5 Kf7 14. Ke4 Bc3 15. Ra7 Kg8 16. Kd5 Bb2 { ( } Bg7 $2 17. Rxg7 $1 Kxg7 18. Ke6 { has to be avoided, in similar fashion toPolgar - Ciocaltea in the 'King first!' chapter) } 17. Ke6 Bc3 ) 
Bxh6 11. Rxa5 Bg7 12. Kf3 Bc3 13. Ra7 Bb2 14. Ke4 ( { If } 14. Kg4 { , then } Bf6 15. h4 ( { or } 15. Rb7 Kf8 $1 { losing a tempo; } ) 
Kf8 $1 16. h5 gxh5 17. Kxh5 Ke8 { and the king moves over to one of the 'right' corners on a8. } ) 
Bc3 15. Kd5 Bb2 16. Ke6 Bc3 17. Rd7 { Timman prepares a frontal attack at g6, but this will let Black's king approach the h4-pawn. Nevertheless, there was no other way of making progress. } Bb2 18. Rd2 Bc3 19. Rg2 Kg7 20. Rg4 Bb2 $1 ( { After the text Timman instead agreed to a draw. Let's make a few more moves: Morozevich of course avoids } 20... Kh6 $2 21. Kf7 { with increased winning chances for White. } ) 
21. h4 Kh6 $1 ( { Now it is right to activate the king, in view of the threat of } 21... Kh6 21. h5 ) 
22. Kf7 Kh5 23. Rxg6 Kxh4 24. Ke6 Kh3 { This time the king is heading for the other safe corner on h1. } 25. Kd5 Kh2 26. Ke4 Kh1 27. Rg5 ( { After } 27. Kf3 Be5 { , the bishop assists successfully in the defence; e.g. } 28. Kf2 Bc7 29. Rg1 ( 29. Rh6 Bh2 { makes no sense; } ) 
Kh2 30. Rg7 Bb6 ) 
Ba3 $1 { Having been denied the e5-square, the bishop instead tries to reach the h2-b8 diagonal via d6. } 28. Kf3 ( 28. Rd5 Kg2 { doesn't achieve much; e.g. } 29. Rd2 Kh1 30. Kf3 Bc5 $1 { and the bishop finds the corresponding diagonal. } ) 
Bd6 29. Kf2 Bc7 { We saw a similar position in the 27.Kf3 subline. Thanks to the location of the king in one of the safe corners and the deployment of the bishop on the h2-b8 diagonal, Black draws. } 
